===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|insect}}
From {{inh|en|enm|flye}}, {{m|enm|flie}}, from {{inh|en|ang|flȳġe}}, {{m|ang|flēoge||a fly}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*fleugǭ||a fly}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*plewk-||to fly}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|flee}}, {{cog|stq|Fljooge}}, {{cog|nl|vlieg}}, {{cog|nds-de|Fleeg}}, {{cog|de|Fliege}}, {{cog|da|flue}}, {{cog|nb|flue}}, {{cog|nn|fluge}}, {{cog|sv|fluga}}, {{cog|is|fluga}}.

====Noun====
[[File:Mouche verte dos.jpg|thumb|A fly (insect)]]

{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|true fly, any insect of order Diptera}} {{lb|en|zoology}} Any [[insect]] of the order [[Diptera]]; characterized by having two wings (except for some wingless species), also called {{vern|true fly|true flies}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-01| author=Douglas Larson| volume=100| issue=1| page=46| magazine=w:American Scientist| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/1/runaway-devils-lake Runaway Devils Lake]| passage=Devils Lake is where I began my career as a limnologist in 1964, studying the lake’s neotenic salamanders and chironomids, or midge '''flies'''. [&hellip;] The Devils Lake Basin is an endorheic, or closed, basin covering about 9,800 square kilometers in northeastern North Dakota.}}
# {{senseid|en|any insect of family Muscidae, nontechnical usage}} {{lb|en|non-technical}} ''Especially'', any of the insects of the family [[Muscidae]], such as the common [[housefly]] (other families of Diptera include [[mosquito]]es and [[midge]]s).
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=When you're well enough off so's you don't have to fret about anything but your heft or your diseases you begin to get queer, I suppose. And the queerer the cure for those ailings the bigger the attraction. A place like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to draw freaks, same as molasses draws '''flies'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|any flying insect, nontechnical usage}} Any similar but not closely related insect, such as a [[dragonfly]], [[butterfly]], or [[gallfly]].
# {{senseid|en|fishing}} {{lb|en|fishing}} A lightweight fishing [[lure]] [[resemble|resembling]] an insect.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad| author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen| title=Folk and Fairy Tales| page=152| passage=I went on trying for fish along the western bank down the river, but only small trout rose at my '''flies''', and a score was the total catch.}}
# {{senseid|en|chest exercise}} {{lb|en|weightlifting}} A [[chest]] [[exercise]] performed by moving extended [[arm]]s from the sides to in front of the [[chest]]. (''also'' [[flye]])
# {{senseid|en|butterfly stroke in swimming}} {{lb|en|swimming}} The [[butterfly stroke]] (''plural is normally'' [[flys]]).
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[witch]]'s [[familiar]].
#* {{RQ:Jonson Alchemist
|passage=a trifling '''fly''', none of your great familiars}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[parasite]]. <!--which sense?-->
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1636|year_published=1845| chapter=w:The Bashful Lover| title=The Plays of Philip Massinger| author=w:Philip Massinger| editor=William Gifford| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6WtVAAAAcAAJ| section=act 1, scene 1| page=470| passage=The '''fly''' that plays too near the flame burns in it.}}
# {{lb|en|preceded by definite article}} A simple [[dance]] in which the hands are [[shake|shaken]] in the air, popular in the 1960s.
# {{lb|en|finance}} A [[butterfly]] (combination of four options).

