===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|flien}}, from {{der|en|ang|flēogan}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*fleuganą}} (compare {{cog|stq|fljooge}}, {{cog|nl|vliegen}}, {{cog|nds|flegen}}, {{cog|de|fliegen}}, {{cog|da|flyve}}, {{cog|nn|flyga}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*plewk-||to fly|tr=*plew-k-}} (compare {{cog|lt|plaukti|plaũkti}} ‘to swim’), enlargement of {{m|ine-pro|*plew-||flow}}. More at [[flee]] and [[flow]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb|flies|flying|flew|flown}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[travel]] through the [[air]], another [[gas]]{{,}} or a [[vacuum]], without being in contact with a grounded surface.
#: {{ux|en|Birds of passage '''fly''' to warmer regions as it gets colder in winter.}}
#: {{ux|en|The Concorde '''flew''' from Paris to New York faster than any other passenger airplane.}}
#: {{ux|en|It takes about eleven hours to '''fly''' from Frankfurt to Hong Kong.}}
#: {{ux|en|The little fairy '''flew''' home on the back of her friend, the giant eagle.}}
#* {{RQ:Chesterton Orthodoxy
|passage=Angels can '''fly''' because they take themselves lightly.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-09-07|volume=408|issue=8852|magazine=w:The Economist|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21584962-small-remote-controlled-craft-powered-sun-are-taking-air On a bright new wing]| passage='''Flying''' using only the power of the sun is an enticing prospect. But manned solar-powered aircraft are fragile and slow,&nbsp;[&hellip;].}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|archaic|poetic}} To [[flee]], to [[escape]] (from).
#: {{ux|en|'''Fly''', my lord! The enemy are upon us!}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=IV|scene=iv|page=56|column=1|passage=[V]pon a [[sudden|ſodaine]], / As ''[[w:Falstaff|Falſtaffe]]'', ſhe, and I, are newly met, / Let them [children dressed like "urchins, [[ouphe]]s and fairies"] from forth a ſaw-pit ruſh at once / With ſome diffuſed ſong: Vpon their ſight / We two, in great amazedneſſe will '''flye''': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|act=3|scene=ii|passage=to '''fly''' the favours of so good a king}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=5|passage='''Fly''', ere evil intercept thy flight.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|14|passage=Sleep '''flies''' the wretch.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=w:Edgar Allan Poe|chapter={{w|Tamerlane (poem)|Tamerlane}}|title=Al Aaraaf, Tamerlane and Minor Poems|passage=And boyhood is a summer sun<br>Whose waning is the dreariest one —<br>For all we live to know is known<br>And all we seek to keep hath '''flown''' — {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1954|author=w:J. R. R. Tolkien|title=w:The Fellowship of the Ring
|passage=He staggered and fell, grasped vainly at the stone, and slid into the abyss. “'''Fly''', you fools!” he cried, and was gone.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To cause to fly {{gloss|travel or float in the air}}: to [[transport]] via air or the like.
#: {{ux|en|Charles Lindbergh '''flew''' his airplane The Spirit of St. Louis across the Atlantic ocean.}}
#: {{ux|en|Why don’t you go outside and '''fly''' kites, kids? The wind is just perfect.}}
#: {{ux|en|Birds '''fly''' their prey to their nest to feed it to their young.}}
#: {{ux|en|Each day the postal service '''flies''' thousands of letters around the globe.}}
#* {{RQ:Gilbert and Sullivan Pirates of Penzance
|passage=The brave black flag I '''fly'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-09-07| volume=408| issue=8852| magazine=w:The Economist| title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21584962-small-remote-controlled-craft-powered-sun-are-taking-air On a bright new wing]| passage=A solar-powered unmanned aerial system (a UAS, more commonly called a drone) could '''fly''' long, lonely missions that conventional aircraft would not be capable of.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Jeromy Hopgood|title=Dance Production: Design and Technology|page=44
|passage=This area, referred to as the fly loft, should typically be two and a half times taller than the proscenium opening in order to '''fly''' the scenery above the vertical sightlines of the first row of the audience.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To travel or proceed very fast; to hasten.
#: ''He '''flew''' down the hill on his bicycle.''
#: ''It's five o'clock already. Doesn't time '''fly'''?''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1645|author=w:John Milton|title=On Time
|passage='''Fly''', envious Time, till thou run out thy race.}}
#* '''1870''', {{w|William Cullen Bryant}} (translator), ''The Iliad'' (originally by {{w|Homer}})
#*: The dark waves murmured as the ship '''flew''' on.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 18, 2011|author=Ben Dirs| work=BBC Sport| title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/14959055.stm Rugby World Cup 2011: England 41-10 Georgia]| passage=After yet another missed penalty by Kvirikashvili from bang in front of the posts, England scored again, centre Tuilagi '''flying''' into the line and touching down under the bar.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move suddenly, or with violence; to do an act suddenly or swiftly.
#: {{ux|en|a door '''flies''' open}}
#: {{ux|en|a bomb '''flies''' apart}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Haunted House|chapter=The Mortals in the House|page=7|column=1|passage=And in respect of the great necessity there is, my darling, for more employments being within the reach of Woman than our civilisation has as yet assigned to her, don’t '''fly''' at the unfortunate men, even those men who are at first sight in your way, as if they were the natural oppressors of your sex {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To proceed with great success.
#: ''His career is really '''flying''' at the moment.''
#: ''One moment the company was '''flying''' high, the next it was on its knees.''
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial|of a proposal, project or idea}} To be [[accepted]], [[come about]] or [[work out]].
#: {{ux|en|Let's see if that idea '''flies'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|You know, I just don't think that's going to '''fly'''. Why don't you spend your time on something better?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To display (a flag) on a flagpole.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[hunt]] with a [[hawk]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1590|author={{w|Robert Greene (dramatist)|Robert Greene}}|title=w:Friar Bacon and Friar Bungay|passage=We'll '''fly''' the partridge, or go rouse the deer.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|entomology|of a type of moth or butterfly}} To be in the winged adult stage.
#: ''This species '''flies''' from late summer until frost''.

